{:edb/db-before-t 1558 :edb/db-after-t 1559 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534871 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:41:27.824Z" 13194139534871 true] [17592186183581 1104 #edb/ref 17592186183584 13194139534871 true] [17592186183997 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = -\\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {15} {17}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac {8} {17}$.\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u + v\\right)$." 13194139534871 false] [17592186183997 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = -\\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = \\dfrac {15} {17}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {8} {17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u + v\\right).$" 13194139534871 true] [17592186183997 1145 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^{2} u &= 1 - \\sin^{2} u \\\\\n&= 1 - (-\\frac{4}{5})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{4^{2}}{5^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{25} - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos u &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (u + v) &= \\cos u\\cos v - \\sin u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{15}{17}) - (-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{45}{85} + \\frac{32}{85} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{13}{85}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 false] [17592186183997 1145 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})-(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}+\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 true] [17592186184079 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad \\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$." 13194139534871 false] [17592186184079 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \n\\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right).$" 13194139534871 true] [17592186184079 1145 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^{2} v &= 1 - \\sin^{2} v \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{9}{15})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{9^{2}}{15^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{81}{225} \\\\\n&= \\frac{225}{225} - \\frac{81}{225} \\\\\n&= \\frac{144}{225}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos v &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{12}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (u - v) &= \\cos u\\cos v + \\sin u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{12}{15}) + (\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{75} + \\frac{36}{75} \\\\\n&= \\frac{72}{75} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 false] [17592186184079 1145 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{9}{15})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{9}^{2}}{{15}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{225}{225}-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{225}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{12}{15})+(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{75}+\\frac{36}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 true] [17592186184087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316339 13194139534871 true] [17592186184087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316349 13194139534871 true] [17592186184087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316363 13194139534871 true] [17592186184087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316368 13194139534871 true] [17592186184087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316373 13194139534871 true] [17592186184170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316346 13194139534871 true] [17592186184170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316352 13194139534871 true] [17592186184170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316358 13194139534871 true] [17592186184782 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534871 true] [17592186184782 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = \\frac{3}{5}, \\cos u = \\frac{4}{5}, \\cos v = \\frac{15}{17}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos (u - v)$." 13194139534871 false] [17592186184782 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" 13194139534871 true] [17592186184782 1145 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{15}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{85}+\\frac{24}{85} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534871 true] [17592186184783 1017 #edb/ref 17592186183587 13194139534871 false] [17592186184783 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316380 13194139534871 true] [17592186184783 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534871 true] [17592186184783 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos u = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}, \\sin v = \\frac{3}{5}, \\cos v = \\frac{4}{5}$.\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n$\\cos (u + v) = \\frac{a\\sqrt{6} - b}{35}$,\ncalculate the value of $a + b$." 13194139534871 false] [17592186184783 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{6}-b}{35},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139534871 true] [17592186184783 1145 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}{{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{49}-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})(\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{5}{7})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}}{35}-\\frac{15}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}-15}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=8,b=15,$ and $a+b=23.$" 13194139534871 true] [17592186316339 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316340 13194139534871 true] [17592186316339 1089 "q-141444" 13194139534871 true] [17592186316339 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534871 true] [17592186316339 1100 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{6}-b\\sqr